# Build the quiz answer endpoint

## Step

Persist quiz answers so correct/incorrect rates are queryable per question.
Execution is authorized (brief Notes, Joel 2026-07-24).

Write to `QuestionResponse` with `fields: { answer, correct }`, per the pinned
finish line. Identity is already settled in
[Decide what identifies an inline quiz](./decide-inline-quiz-identity.svx) — do
not invent it here:

- `surveyId` = the lesson's content resource id
- `questionId` = the `sync_question_<hash>` id minted by
  [sync extraction](./build-sync-question-extraction.svx)

What the endpoint has to get right:

- **Identity is user-first** (Joel, 2026-07-25: "for these quizzes they will have
  to be logged in, generally"). This supersedes the anonymous-primary assumption
  in [Decide what identifies an inline quiz](./decide-inline-quiz-identity.svx),
  which was pinned before we knew learners would typically be authenticated.
  The old design had a real defect under the new assumption: with `respondentKey`
  minted from a browser cookie, one logged-in learner on laptop and phone becomes
  two respondents, gets two rows for the same question, and double-counts in
  correct-rate analytics. So `respondentKey` derives from `userId` when a session
  exists and falls back to the cookie session id when it does not.
  `surveySessionId` stays the browser session in both cases — `respondentKey`
  answers "who", `surveySessionId` answers "which browser".
  **Settled (Joel, 2026-07-25): gate the saving, not the answering.**
  Answering stays open to everyone — a logged-out learner still gets graded and
  still sees the explanation, with no auth wall and no new UI. Only persistence
  requires a session. Chosen as the simplest of the three: it reuses the guard
  that already skips posting for a missing `id` or `lessonId`, so it adds one
  condition rather than a component, and it deletes the whole anonymous identity
  branch server-side. The hard gate looked simpler but was the only option
  needing NEW UI — deciding and building what a logged-out visitor sees.
  Consequence: `respondentKey` is always `user:<userId>`; cookie minting, the
  `session:` fallback, and the anonymous-then-login transition are all removed.
  Logged-out answers are not recorded at all, which keeps the free top-of-funnel
  open while making the data strictly per-user.
- ~~**Anonymous learners must work.**~~ **Superseded 2026-07-25** by the
  gate-the-saving decision above. Anonymous learners answer but are not stored,
  so there is no anonymous respondent identity to define. `respondentKey` is
  always `user:<userId>`.
- **Upgrade identity when it resolves.** Follow the existing resolution order:
  NextAuth session, then `ck_subscriber_id` / `ck_subscriber` cookie, then
  nothing (`convertkit.ts:140-143`, `convertkit.ts:448-500`). A learner who logs
  in later should not fork into two respondents if that is cheaply avoidable; if
  it is not cheap, say so and accept it explicitly.
- **The unique index is your upsert key.**
  `(surveyId, questionId, respondentKey)` is a unique index
  (`question-response.ts:56-58`), so a resubmit updates rather than duplicates.
  Use it deliberately; do not let a retry mint a second row.
- **Grade server-side from the question row.** This used to be the hard part of
  the step; sync extraction solved it. The client posts `{ questionId, answer }`
  and the server reads `correct` from the `sync_question_<hash>` content resource
  and decides. No HMAC, no client-supplied boolean, no MDX re-parse. Never accept
  a `correct` value from the client.
- A posted `questionId` with no matching question resource is an error, not a
  silent insert — it means sync has not run or the body drifted.
- Grade with the shared `gradeAnswer` function, the same module the component
  imports. Multi-select is required, so `answer` may be an array and the match is
  exact-set, all or nothing
  ([Decide multi-select handling](./decide-multi-select-in-the-inline-quiz.svx)).
  `QuestionResponse.fields` is JSON, so an array answer needs no schema change.
- **No Kit on the answer path at all** (Joel, 2026-07-25: "these quizzes should
  store in our db not kit"). Answers live in `QuestionResponse` and nothing is
  written to Kit — that was always true. What changed: identity resolution must
  not *call* Kit either. `emailListProvider.getSubscriber()` is removed from this
  path, because the round-trip fetched an id we already hold — the
  `ck_subscriber_id` cookie IS the subscriber id, so `emailListSubscriberId` is
  set straight from the cookie. Accepted loss: the
  `subscriber.email_address` → `users.findFirst` inference that mapped a
  logged-out Kit subscriber to a `userId`. ~~`respondentKey` still ties that
  learner's answers together and `userId` fills in on real login.~~
  **Superseded 2026-07-25 by gate-the-saving:** logged-out answers are not stored,
  so there is no anonymous identity continuity; every saved row has a `userId`
  and a `respondentKey` derived from it. No replacement lookup, no external calls
  on the answer path.
- **Do not mirror to Kit.** Surveys write answers into Kit subscriber fields
  (`convertkit.ts:157-180`, `surveys-query.ts:587-619`). Whether quizzes should is
  an open latent question on the brief, not a decision for this step. Default to
  not doing it.
- Per the observability rule: ship per-stage structured logs and one command that
  traces a single answer end to end, before this goes live.

